WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.719
AMD GPU systems use a different ecosystem.

00:00:02.719 --> 00:00:07.209
The AMDGPU kernel driver provides core Linux support for many AMD devices,

00:00:07.209 --> 00:00:10.951
while ROCm supports GPU compute workflows at a high level.

00:00:10.951 --> 00:00:16.189
HIP is a programming model associated with portable GPU compute in the AMD ecosystem.

00:00:16.189 --> 00:00:19.930
Support depends on hardware generation, distribution, kernel version, user-space components,

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:21.053
and application compatibility.

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:25.917
Vulkan compute paths may be useful for certain local inference or graphics-adjacent workloads,

00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:28.536
especially when a runtime supports Vulkan acceleration.

00:00:28.536 --> 00:00:33.026
Mesa and related user-space graphics components can also matter for integrated GPUs,

00:00:33.026 --> 00:00:35.271
desktop systems, and open driver paths.

00:00:35.271 --> 00:00:39.387
These options are not interchangeable with CUDA, and application support varies.

00:00:39.387 --> 00:00:42.380
Integrated GPUs and discrete GPUs have different expectations.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:47.992
Integrated GPUs may share system memory and be useful for lightweight workloads or display tasks.

00:00:47.992 --> 00:00:51.360
Discrete GPUs usually provide stronger acceleration and dedicated VRAM,

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.727
but they also require power, cooling, and driver compatibility.

00:00:54.727 --> 00:00:59.217
Some systems may use CPU fallback when GPU acceleration is not available

00:00:59.217 --> 00:01:01.088
or not worth the complexity.

00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:06.326
The practical decision is to match the compute path to the workload and hardware.

00:01:06.326 --> 00:01:10.816
ROCm may be appropriate when the GPU and distribution are supported and

00:01:10.816 --> 00:01:12.687
the AI framework expects it.

00:01:12.687 --> 00:01:15.680
Vulkan may be enough for certain local runtimes.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.796
CPU fallback may be acceptable for small models or low-volume tasks.

00:01:19.796 --> 00:01:24.660
The right path is the one that works reliably for the actual system.
